# EVERY TEXT FILE CHECKS OUT LF, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# The Windows runners (and many Windows clones) set core.autocrlf=true, and on a
# CRLF checkout most of the tests that read a file as text fail. A file whose
# exact bytes are under test needs its own `-text` line BELOW this one: the
# later line wins.
* text=auto eol=lf

# THE UPDATER FIXTURES ARE BYTES, NOT TEXT.
#
# `src-tauri/src/updater_tests.rs` verifies a committed minisign signature over a
# committed payload. The payload is 169 bytes of which exactly one is a newline,
# and it contains no NUL — so git's own heuristic classifies it as text, and a
# checkout on Windows (where the runners set core.autocrlf=true) rewrites that
# one byte to CRLF. The file is then 170 bytes, the signature no longer covers
# it, and `valid_payload_is_accepted` fails on the assert.
#
# That failure reads as "the pubkey and the fixture disagree", which is the one
# thing it is not: the same commit passes on macOS and Linux and fails only on
# Windows, because only Windows changed the bytes on the way in.
#
# `-text` means never convert, in either direction, on any platform. It is the
# only correct setting for a file whose exact bytes are the thing under test.
apps/desktop/test/fixtures/updater/* -text
